WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.958 --> 00:00:06.420
Scary fun was the motto for one of
the startups I worked for back in the day.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.543
Many who choose to create and

00:00:08.543 --> 00:00:12.529
build organizations can
surely relate to this idea.

00:00:12.529 --> 00:00:14.382
Hello, I'm Matt Abrahams, and

00:00:14.382 --> 00:00:18.624
I teach Strategic Communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:18.624 --> 00:00:23.058
Welcome to Think Fast,
Talk Smart, The Podcast.

00:00:23.058 --> 00:00:27.731
I am incredibly excited and
honored today to chat with Steve Blank.

00:00:27.731 --> 00:00:32.272
Steve is an adjunct professor at Stanford
in the Department of Management, Science,

00:00:32.272 --> 00:00:33.277
and Engineering.

00:00:33.277 --> 00:00:38.043
He teaches courses on lean startups,
innovation, and entrepreneurship.

00:00:38.043 --> 00:00:42.203
He is credited with launching
the Lean startup movement, and

00:00:42.203 --> 00:00:45.643
he's the author of
The Four Steps to the Epiphany,

00:00:45.643 --> 00:00:49.726
The Startup Owner's Manual,
and Holding a Cat by its Tail.

00:00:49.726 --> 00:00:50.935
Welcome, Steve.

00:00:50.935 --> 00:00:52.633
&gt;&gt; Thanks for having me, Matt.

00:00:52.633 --> 00:00:55.719
&gt;&gt; You've been an entrepreneur for
over 45 years,

00:00:55.719 --> 00:01:00.325
founding or co-founding eight businesses
and helping countless others.

00:01:00.325 --> 00:01:04.617
You mentioned this lean startup
methodology, can you summarize that

00:01:04.617 --> 00:01:09.502
approach and what it looks like and what
are some best practices included in it?

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:14.002
&gt;&gt; Well, Matt, that's usually a semester
class, but I'll try to do it.

00:01:14.002 --> 00:01:14.796
[LAUGH] Don't cut me off.

00:01:14.796 --> 00:01:18.715
&gt;&gt; Right, we'll take the cliffnotes
version, if you don't mind.

00:01:18.715 --> 00:01:23.575
&gt;&gt; The cliffnotes version basically says,
and it's so obvious it's like duh,

00:01:23.575 --> 00:01:25.637
but we didn't quite process it,

00:01:25.637 --> 00:01:30.218
is that there are no facts inside your
building, so get the heck outside.

00:01:30.218 --> 00:01:31.872
&gt;&gt; Right?
&gt;&gt; And what I mean by that is,

00:01:31.872 --> 00:01:36.475
if you remember, what I said is startups
used to believe it's all they needed to do

00:01:36.475 --> 00:01:40.101
is write a plan and raise some money and
now execute for the plan.

00:01:40.101 --> 00:01:40.760
Why?

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:46.101
Because if the plan was funded, well,
VCs and God have blessed that plan.

00:01:46.101 --> 00:01:48.295
Those aren't hypotheses,
those are facts, right?

00:01:48.295 --> 00:01:50.699
People give you money for
them, so let's go build it.

00:01:50.699 --> 00:01:55.054
The lean method simply says, look,
you have a set of hypotheses.

00:01:55.054 --> 00:01:59.325
It's okay, you're out there on day one,
a start up is a faith-based enterprise.

00:01:59.325 --> 00:02:02.738
But we want to turn it into
facts as rapidly as possible.

00:02:02.738 --> 00:02:07.066
And no facts exist inside your building,
or very few.

00:02:07.066 --> 00:02:08.830
And if you're in sales or marketing,

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:12.690
it's impossible to be smarter than the
collective intelligence of your potential

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:14.467
customers sitting in your building.

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:17.406
So let's go out and test these hypotheses.

00:02:17.406 --> 00:02:21.073
So piece one of the lean startup is
a formal methodology called customer

00:02:21.073 --> 00:02:21.876
development.

00:02:21.876 --> 00:02:25.497
Let's go do these things to
validate who are the customers,

00:02:25.497 --> 00:02:29.972
what features do they want, [INAUDIBLE]
do they want, competitors, etc.

00:02:29.972 --> 00:02:34.160
But while we're doing that, something
else evolved in the 21st century.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.669
And that's the way to build products
using something called Agile engineering.

00:02:38.669 --> 00:02:41.962
That is, let's build products
iteratively and incrementally,

00:02:41.962 --> 00:02:45.252
rather than using a waterfall method,
which is a write this spec,

00:02:45.252 --> 00:02:49.157
build the product, go through alpha test,
beta test, first customership.

00:02:49.157 --> 00:02:53.537
If you use Agile instead, you could
be building wireframes, or tests, or

00:02:53.537 --> 00:02:57.779
spreadsheets, or something to put in
front of customers very early on,

00:02:57.779 --> 00:03:00.782
or potential customers,
and get their feedback.

00:03:00.782 --> 00:03:03.475
And then take that back and
say, well, wait a minute,

00:03:03.475 --> 00:03:05.066
were the assumptions correct?

00:03:05.066 --> 00:03:08.767
So Agile engineering is part
two of the lean startup.

00:03:08.767 --> 00:03:11.909
And then finally,
what hypotheses should you be testing?

00:03:11.909 --> 00:03:13.266
I just mentioned some of them,

00:03:13.266 --> 00:03:16.398
it turns out that someone named
Alexander Osterwalder had put together

00:03:16.398 --> 00:03:18.450
something called
the Business Model Canvas.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:22.167
And that's a pretty good roadmap of the
nine things you need to worry about when

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:23.760
you're building a new venture.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.080
Not all of them, but
it gets about 80 to 90%.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:31.172
Now, in fact, Lean is those three
components, customer development,

00:03:31.172 --> 00:03:33.950
agile engineering, and
business model design.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:37.400
&gt;&gt; That was an amazing crash course,
thank you.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:38.703
&gt;&gt; You got the whole semester.

00:03:38.703 --> 00:03:39.718
&gt;&gt; There we go.

00:03:39.718 --> 00:03:41.083
&gt;&gt; Nobody needs to take my class.

00:03:41.083 --> 00:03:44.550
&gt;&gt; I was just going to say, send me
the final exam, I'm ready to graduate.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.883
I like the way you package that up.

00:03:46.883 --> 00:03:51.382
And faith to fact, I think, is a really
interesting way to think about it.

00:03:51.382 --> 00:03:55.558
Generating hypotheses, testing them with
people who actually might want to buy your

00:03:55.558 --> 00:03:57.250
product, what a brilliant idea.

00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:03.224
And then this notion of rapid iteration
and prototyping, critical as well.

00:04:03.224 --> 00:04:06.208
I have a hypothesis I'd
like to run by you.

00:04:06.208 --> 00:04:10.759
So part of what you discussed as
the lean methodology is this notion of

00:04:10.759 --> 00:04:15.545
minimally viable product design where
you test for user requirements,

00:04:15.545 --> 00:04:19.939
you understand, at least have
some hypotheses about the market,

00:04:19.939 --> 00:04:23.802
come up with some wireframes,
and then test and iterate.

00:04:23.802 --> 00:04:28.976
I feel that same process can apply
to our communication as well.

00:04:28.976 --> 00:04:32.280
It's about, as you said,
understanding your audience.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.996
It's about understanding the context in
which the messages need to be created and

00:04:36.996 --> 00:04:37.688
deployed.

00:04:37.688 --> 00:04:39.640
It's about structuring those messages,

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:42.850
much like you would build
a wireframe of a product or service.

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:44.961
And then you iterate and test.

00:04:44.961 --> 00:04:50.463
Does the concept of minimally viable
communication resonate with you at all?

00:04:50.463 --> 00:04:54.322
&gt;&gt; I think that's a key tenet
of everything I teach you about

00:04:54.322 --> 00:04:55.617
communications.

00:04:55.617 --> 00:05:00.668
For a startup, and I don't mean to diss
on these folks, but hiring a PR agency

00:05:00.668 --> 00:05:05.690
too early is a sign that the CEO is
treating this as someone else's problem.

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:08.186
For me, in a startup, the first task,

00:05:08.186 --> 00:05:12.350
the minimum viable product of
understanding audience message,

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:17.410
media and messenger can only be done
with the founders and CEO engaged.

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:21.422
That is, even if they think they know
nothing about any of this stuff,

00:05:21.422 --> 00:05:25.583
I want them to actually get out of
the building and figure this out, why?

00:05:25.583 --> 00:05:30.922
Because they'll get it wrong and whatever,
but they'll have a fingertip feel now of,

00:05:30.922 --> 00:05:35.656
this is our stake in the ground, now let's
hire professionals who could do this.

00:05:35.656 --> 00:05:38.126
But we've built our own MVPs with,
in fact,

00:05:38.126 --> 00:05:42.556
the people who best know the product and
think they know the rest of these things.

00:05:42.556 --> 00:05:45.693
It makes a much better
relationship with a agency and

00:05:45.693 --> 00:05:50.327
the founders, who typically never get
engaged in this part of the business,

00:05:50.327 --> 00:05:53.052
to actually have to have
done it themselves.

00:05:53.052 --> 00:05:55.345
And I don't mean six months of this, but

00:05:55.345 --> 00:05:58.324
I mean a couple of weeks of
trying to figure this out.

00:05:58.324 --> 00:06:04.880
I make them draft those audience message
messengers and media draft themselves.

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:07.728
And then when they're interviewing
an agency, they could say, well,

00:06:07.728 --> 00:06:08.950
this is what we found.

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:11.513
Tell us what's wrong, or
how do we do this better?

00:06:11.513 --> 00:06:14.607
And usually good agencies
could add huge value here, but

00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:18.735
at least they're talking to an educated
founding team who appreciates what

00:06:18.735 --> 00:06:20.618
the language means and the value.

00:06:20.618 --> 00:06:24.112
&gt;&gt; Excellent, so
getting your hands dirty as a founder and

00:06:24.112 --> 00:06:29.168
really understanding the impact and
importance of that communication can serve

00:06:29.168 --> 00:06:33.945
you well when you then go get professional
help to help move things forward.

00:06:33.945 --> 00:06:35.941
&gt;&gt; And let me double down why.

00:06:35.941 --> 00:06:39.803
Because smart CEOs treat
communications as a force multiplier,

00:06:39.803 --> 00:06:43.102
a tool to dramatically increase sales or
valuation, or

00:06:43.102 --> 00:06:46.064
the vehicles to get acquired
lined up at the door.

00:06:46.064 --> 00:06:50.652
Not so successful CEOs treat it as
a tactic that could be handed to others.

00:06:50.652 --> 00:06:53.889
And that's the difference for
me about where marketing and

00:06:53.889 --> 00:06:57.721
marketing communications needs to
sit in the early days of a company.

00:06:57.721 --> 00:06:59.541
If the founders never been engage,

00:06:59.541 --> 00:07:02.450
you're never going to get
high quality communications.

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:07.447
And I think the canonical or
the pinnacle of this was Steve Jobs.

00:07:07.447 --> 00:07:08.600
He was engaged.

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:12.530
This to him wasn't some other thing
that you handed to someone else,

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:14.157
he was constantly engaged,

00:07:14.157 --> 00:07:18.996
and Apple had some of the most brilliant
marketing of the 20th and 21st century.

00:07:18.996 --> 00:07:21.243
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, and
it still reverberates today.

00:07:21.243 --> 00:07:24.530
I'd like to dig a little deeper
into the role of communication.

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:28.595
And how have you seen communication
played not just in marketing and

00:07:28.595 --> 00:07:33.602
selling of products and ideas, but within
organizations in terms of teams, etc.

00:07:33.602 --> 00:07:38.228
What best practices or advice and guidance
have you seen that can really make

00:07:38.228 --> 00:07:40.982
a difference with regard to communication?

00:07:40.982 --> 00:07:42.380
&gt;&gt; Boy, do we have another semester?

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:46.960
I mean, [LAUGH] stop me
when we run out of airtime.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:52.439
But let me start with, even today, people
tend to make the same mistakes about

00:07:52.439 --> 00:07:58.182
marketing and communication in startups
as I did when I was a young entrepreneur.

00:07:58.182 --> 00:08:02.504
Number one is they often
confuse communications tactics.

00:08:02.504 --> 00:08:06.971
What should my webpage look like,
or should I be using Facebook or

00:08:06.971 --> 00:08:09.093
Instagram, with a strategy.

00:08:09.093 --> 00:08:14.520
A communication strategy, at least for
me, answers a couple questions.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:16.800
Why are we doing these activities?

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:20.985
For example, and I'll give you four
examples, these happen to be my four, but

00:08:20.985 --> 00:08:24.984
we could make us some others is,
is there a communication strategy designed

00:08:24.984 --> 00:08:28.690
to create demand for our products and
drive them into a sales channel?

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:32.800
And are we trying to create awareness
of our company and brand for

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:37.385
potential customers, are we try to
educate and position the company?

00:08:37.385 --> 00:08:39.702
Are we trying to create awareness for
fundraising?

00:08:39.702 --> 00:08:41.852
That is, gee, we're out raising money and

00:08:41.852 --> 00:08:44.830
we're hoping that we could get
the attention of some folks.

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:47.414
Or do we want to create awareness for
potential acquirers?

00:08:47.414 --> 00:08:52.136
So once you figured out the why
you're creating this strategy,

00:08:52.136 --> 00:08:54.330
I just gave you a list of mine.

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:56.467
And then you need to figure out the how.

00:08:56.467 --> 00:08:58.267
There's just four steps.

00:08:58.267 --> 00:09:00.515
One is understand your audience.

00:09:00.515 --> 00:09:05.330
Number two is, what's the message for
that specific audience?

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:07.922
So we got audience and we got message.

00:09:07.922 --> 00:09:13.283
Three is, what's the media you want the
message to be read or seen or heard on?

00:09:13.283 --> 00:09:18.854
And then four is, who's the messenger
you want to carry your message?

00:09:18.854 --> 00:09:23.313
&gt;&gt; I think that's really, really helpful
to break it down into those ways.

00:09:23.313 --> 00:09:27.953
There's a very simple model of
communication that I will teach, and

00:09:27.953 --> 00:09:30.680
others, that looks at those elements.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:35.047
It's you have a sender, and who is
that sender and who's the best sender?

00:09:35.047 --> 00:09:36.184
What's the message?

00:09:36.184 --> 00:09:37.656
What's the appropriate channel?

00:09:37.656 --> 00:09:42.388
And what can you do to make the likelihood
that your message will resonate

00:09:42.388 --> 00:09:44.259
the most with the receiver?

00:09:44.259 --> 00:09:47.683
And I hear you talking about all
of those elements, for sure.

00:09:47.683 --> 00:09:52.240
&gt;&gt; With one addition, and the one addition
I start with is, so who's the audience?

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:56.803
Because in a startup, there's multiple
audiences typically in your head.

00:09:56.803 --> 00:10:00.653
Do I want to reach the customers,
or do I want to reach VCs, or

00:10:00.653 --> 00:10:02.945
do I want to reach other companies?

00:10:02.945 --> 00:10:06.936
And what's confusing is,
when you're trying to figure this out,

00:10:06.936 --> 00:10:11.151
the way I tend to say, well, timeout,
let's go back to our strategy.

00:10:11.151 --> 00:10:14.674
Are we trying to reach customers or
investors or acquirers?

00:10:14.674 --> 00:10:19.346
And they're very different audiences with
each requiring their own message media and

00:10:19.346 --> 00:10:20.117
messengers.

00:10:20.117 --> 00:10:23.526
And if you try to kind of put them
together, you end up with a ball of twine.

00:10:23.526 --> 00:10:28.150
So how do you figure out which one of
these are most important in what order?

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:32.763
It turns out, if you remember,
we talked about the business model.

00:10:32.763 --> 00:10:37.126
If you drill down on it, you kind of
understood that there are typically

00:10:37.126 --> 00:10:41.708
multiple customer segments, each one
of them who have their own pains that

00:10:41.708 --> 00:10:44.564
they want solved or
are looking for solution.

00:10:44.564 --> 00:10:47.257
VCs are looking for
hot deals in this area.

00:10:47.257 --> 00:10:50.995
Corporate customers might be looking for
something very specific.

00:10:50.995 --> 00:10:54.850
And so to figure out who the audience is,
you need to go back,

00:10:54.850 --> 00:11:00.005
all the way to the back, to what's our
strategy and what's our business model?

00:11:00.005 --> 00:11:01.852
The second part is, what's the message?

00:11:01.852 --> 00:11:05.324
I tend to think that messages
answer three questions.

00:11:05.324 --> 00:11:07.456
Why should the audience care?

00:11:07.456 --> 00:11:08.357
What are you offering?

00:11:08.357 --> 00:11:11.252
And more importantly,
what's the call to action?

00:11:11.252 --> 00:11:14.141
The last one is to me the most important,
it's like, okay,

00:11:14.141 --> 00:11:15.452
what do you want them to do?

00:11:15.452 --> 00:11:19.188
You want to pick up the phone, or
click here, or do something else?

00:11:19.188 --> 00:11:23.754
Or are you just trying to kind of get into
their heads and you could own a space?

00:11:23.754 --> 00:11:28.099
Now what's nice is if you've been doing
some of this customer development that's

00:11:28.099 --> 00:11:32.381
getting out in the building, this is a big
idea is that your customers have already

00:11:32.381 --> 00:11:35.544
told you how to craft the first
part of the message, that is,

00:11:35.544 --> 00:11:36.743
why they should care.

00:11:36.743 --> 00:11:41.849
That comes directly from the, if you've
done customer development correctly,

00:11:41.849 --> 00:11:44.961
they've told you what their pains and
gains are.

00:11:44.961 --> 00:11:48.295
If you haven't done any of this getting
out of the building, then you're staring

00:11:48.295 --> 00:11:51.067
at a blank sheet of paper going,
I wonder what they care about.

00:11:51.067 --> 00:11:55.300
&gt;&gt; Many things you've said are absolutely
critical to all types of communication.

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:56.769
You have to know your audience.

00:11:56.769 --> 00:11:58.860
You have to understand
what's relevant to them.

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:01.803
Then you have to craft
messages that are tailored

00:12:01.803 --> 00:12:05.547
to that audience that are in
service of the broader strategy.

00:12:05.547 --> 00:12:11.074
And one key point that I really want to
echo for everybody to really internalize

00:12:11.074 --> 00:12:15.678
is, you have to adjust and
adapt the message for that audience.

00:12:15.678 --> 00:12:20.320
It is so often that people try to create
one pitch deck or one message and

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:23.165
then spread it among multiple audiences.

00:12:23.165 --> 00:12:25.427
And that's where you dilute the message,
and

00:12:25.427 --> 00:12:28.216
you can actually confuse
the people you're talking to.

00:12:28.216 --> 00:12:31.061
So that point you made,
all the points were valuable, but

00:12:31.061 --> 00:12:33.340
that one in particular
I wanted to highlight.

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:37.427
&gt;&gt; There's a couple more things about the
message that I run into all the time, but

00:12:37.427 --> 00:12:42.012
particularly entrepreneurs coming out of a
technical world, is what are you offering?

00:12:42.012 --> 00:12:45.244
And almost always they
start with a feature list.

00:12:45.244 --> 00:12:49.215
Here are all the bullets, and
here's why we're doing X or Y.

00:12:49.215 --> 00:12:51.015
And I just kind of shut them down.

00:12:51.015 --> 00:12:54.977
I go, wait a minute, if we've gotten out
of the building and talked to customers,

00:12:54.977 --> 00:12:57.026
we should know what's important to them.

00:12:57.026 --> 00:13:02.280
It's not the feature list, but what
relieves pain or creates gain for them.

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:04.911
And again, if you've just sitting in
the building staring at a blank sheet

00:13:04.911 --> 00:13:06.387
of paper, you're not going to have a clue.

00:13:06.387 --> 00:13:08.062
But if you've talked to them,
you would have known it.

00:13:08.062 --> 00:13:09.803
And then finally, the call to action.

00:13:09.803 --> 00:13:12.401
As I said, what do you want them to do?

00:13:12.401 --> 00:13:15.444
The other thing is context for messages.

00:13:15.444 --> 00:13:20.419
A message that's brilliant today might be
met with blank stares two years ago or

00:13:20.419 --> 00:13:22.190
two years from now.

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:25.531
So remember that all
messages operate in context,

00:13:25.531 --> 00:13:28.412
that it sometimes have an expiration date.

00:13:28.412 --> 00:13:31.993
And then finally, messages need to be
memorable, they need to be sticky.

00:13:31.993 --> 00:13:36.460
Because the more memorable the message,
the greater its ability to create change.

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:40.248
The classic analogy is, consider if you
were told you were going to pay for

00:13:40.248 --> 00:13:43.673
cold dead fish wrapped in seaweed,
you might not be too hungry.

00:13:43.673 --> 00:13:47.116
But when we call it sushi,
people line up and pay lots of money.

00:13:47.116 --> 00:13:49.339
So that's enough about message.

00:13:49.339 --> 00:13:50.479
Matt, back to you.

00:13:50.479 --> 00:13:54.597
&gt;&gt; When I'm hearing you say about
messages are so many valuable things.

00:13:54.597 --> 00:13:58.670
One, it has to be tailored to
an audience and their specific needs.

00:13:58.670 --> 00:14:02.249
Second, it should not just
be a list of features,

00:14:02.249 --> 00:14:04.814
it needs to be value and relevance.

00:14:04.814 --> 00:14:08.242
Bullets kill, don't kill your
audience with bullet points.

00:14:08.242 --> 00:14:10.881
You must consider the context as well, and

00:14:10.881 --> 00:14:15.368
the context in which the message is
delivered can change dramatically.

00:14:15.368 --> 00:14:19.385
And then finally, your last point was we
need to make it memorable so it sticks.

00:14:19.385 --> 00:14:23.590
And that has to do with not only
relevance, but the words you use,

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:25.362
the framing you use, etc.

00:14:25.362 --> 00:14:28.075
All of that is such valuable advice,
thank you.

00:14:28.075 --> 00:14:32.748
&gt;&gt; And Matt, the last two points,
go back were media messengers.

00:14:32.748 --> 00:14:35.845
Media today, when I was an entrepreneur,

00:14:35.845 --> 00:14:39.371
we used to cut down dead trees and
put ink on them,

00:14:39.371 --> 00:14:44.294
people used to have antennas on
their roofs and watch broadcast TV.

00:14:44.294 --> 00:14:49.766
Today we just kind of think of social
media, but you need to have a media mix.

00:14:49.766 --> 00:14:52.781
It's likely that each audience
reads different media,

00:14:52.781 --> 00:14:56.811
potential customers read something
different then potential investors.

00:14:56.811 --> 00:14:59.120
You need to have a media strategy, and

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:03.025
that's a plan that describes the mix
of media and how you use it.

00:15:03.025 --> 00:15:07.563
And by the way, the media to me also
includes your website, your Facebook page,

00:15:07.563 --> 00:15:09.050
your Twitter page, etc.

00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:12.171
It should be the first place that you
experiment finding your audience and

00:15:12.171 --> 00:15:12.783
messengers.

00:15:12.783 --> 00:15:17.237
And then the last part is, messengers,
and these are kind of the well placed and

00:15:17.237 --> 00:15:20.716
highly leveraged individuals
who influence your audience.

00:15:20.716 --> 00:15:25.140
And I'm not talking about your CEO,
I'm talking about who's respected

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:29.920
in that industry that now you've actually
given them your PowerPoint slide and

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:32.654
they they're using it
to diagram the space.

00:15:32.654 --> 00:15:34.465
Messengers are the one who convey and

00:15:34.465 --> 00:15:38.099
amplify your message to your audience
through the media you've chosen.

00:15:38.099 --> 00:15:43.053
And so some types of messengers, for
example, are reporters, industry experts,

00:15:43.053 --> 00:15:48.247
evangelists, connectors, and each audience
has their own unique set of messengers.

00:15:48.247 --> 00:15:52.639
And some of them nowadays, social
media can be bought, like influencers.

00:15:52.639 --> 00:15:55.747
So you could actually pay for
some messengers as well.

00:15:55.747 --> 00:16:00.113
And depending on your industry and
what you're trying to achieve,

00:16:00.113 --> 00:16:04.593
messengers will vary very wildly,
&gt;&gt; Very useful reminders there that your

00:16:04.593 --> 00:16:08.945
communication is not just the messages
that you create, it's the media and

00:16:08.945 --> 00:16:11.399
channels through which you release them.

00:16:11.399 --> 00:16:16.042
And it's the messengers who act on your
behalf to amplify and spread them, very,

00:16:16.042 --> 00:16:17.062
very important.

00:16:17.062 --> 00:16:18.106
So before we end,

00:16:18.106 --> 00:16:22.357
I'd like to ask you the same three
questions I ask everyone who joins me.

00:16:22.357 --> 00:16:23.702
You okay with that?

00:16:23.702 --> 00:16:24.225
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:16:24.225 --> 00:16:25.818
&gt;&gt; All right, here we go.

00:16:25.818 --> 00:16:31.428
If you were to capture the best
communication advice you ever received

00:16:31.428 --> 00:16:36.858
as a five to seven word presentation
slide title, what would it be?

00:16:36.858 --> 00:16:41.715
&gt;&gt; Well, it's the one I kind of lived on,
is any idiot can make a complicated thing

00:16:41.715 --> 00:16:45.780
sound complicated, it takes real
skill to make it sound simple.

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:49.603
&gt;&gt; So it takes real skill to make
something sound simple, absolutely.

00:16:49.603 --> 00:16:54.446
We've spent a lot of time on this podcast
with many guests talking about how to make

00:16:54.446 --> 00:16:57.406
complex information
accessible to an audience.

00:16:57.406 --> 00:17:01.807
So it's not about dumbing it down,
it's about making it relevant and

00:17:01.807 --> 00:17:05.174
putting it in into terms
that people can understand.

00:17:05.174 --> 00:17:10.974
Let me ask you question number two,
who is a communicator that you admire and

00:17:10.974 --> 00:17:13.602
why
&gt;&gt; The individual communicator, obviously,

00:17:13.602 --> 00:17:16.811
again, I grew up in a world of
the 20th century Steve Jobs and

00:17:16.811 --> 00:17:18.498
the 21st century Steve Jobs.

00:17:18.498 --> 00:17:23.175
Probably the best in my mind about being
able to create a reality distortion field

00:17:23.175 --> 00:17:27.095
that made you want to throw out
a perfectly good product every year and

00:17:27.095 --> 00:17:28.153
buy the next one.

00:17:28.153 --> 00:17:33.951
Steve Jobs reinvented the 20th
century automobile model change.

00:17:33.951 --> 00:17:37.861
It used to be in the 50s and
60s people would stand in line to

00:17:37.861 --> 00:17:41.473
see the next year's car model,
hard to believe now, and

00:17:41.473 --> 00:17:45.779
actually buy it because it had
bigger tail fins or flashier chrome.

00:17:45.779 --> 00:17:49.907
Steve Jobs essentially resurrected that
idea and managed to convince people that

00:17:49.907 --> 00:17:53.375
the last year's iPhone that they
loved is completely obsolete, but

00:17:53.375 --> 00:17:57.298
you should still love the company,
because this one has a flashier feature.

00:17:57.298 --> 00:17:59.511
The phrase was planned obsolescence.

00:17:59.511 --> 00:18:04.102
And at the same time, obviously, was able
to communicate the benefits of new product

00:18:04.102 --> 00:18:06.547
breakthroughs like iPod and
iPhone and etc.

00:18:06.547 --> 00:18:11.007
&gt;&gt; Final question,
what are the first three ingredients that

00:18:11.007 --> 00:18:14.406
go into a successful communication recipe?

00:18:14.406 --> 00:18:18.065
&gt;&gt; I mentioned kind of the audience
message or message, etc.

00:18:18.065 --> 00:18:21.233
But I guess I would tell you
it's something different,

00:18:21.233 --> 00:18:22.926
to kind of just put a cap on it.

00:18:22.926 --> 00:18:25.224
If the CEO thinks this is a tactic,

00:18:25.224 --> 00:18:29.201
there isn't going to be
a successful communication recipe.

00:18:29.201 --> 00:18:33.809
If the CEO thinks it's a strategy,
an integral and a force multiplier for

00:18:33.809 --> 00:18:38.717
the company, then there's going to be
a successful communication strategy and

00:18:38.717 --> 00:18:39.331
recipe.

00:18:39.331 --> 00:18:42.553
&gt;&gt; Right, so it's really the investment
and focus on communication.

00:18:42.553 --> 00:18:46.718
So many entrepreneurs see
communication as a necessary evil,

00:18:46.718 --> 00:18:52.074
rather than as something that really needs
to have some deep strategy behind it.

00:18:52.074 --> 00:18:53.184
Thank you so much.

00:18:53.184 --> 00:18:58.350
Your insights, perspectives, and passion
are incredibly helpful and motivational.

00:18:58.350 --> 00:19:00.871
I've thoroughly enjoyed our time together,
and

00:19:00.871 --> 00:19:03.400
all of us have learned
a lot from your experience.

00:19:03.400 --> 00:19:04.186
Thank you.

00:19:04.186 --> 00:19:07.954
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you, Matt, I learned a lot,
actually, when you asked the question.

00:19:07.954 --> 00:19:09.422
&gt;&gt; Well, excellent, thank you.

